   If FERT alone carries the gap, the fix is the rain-fed suitability score —
   substituting irrigation for rainfall in the BASE, not multiplying it after.
   If cap÷fert differs too, something downstream (access, relief, dev) is also
   favouring one region, and the substitution alone would not close it.

  WHICH TERM IS STARVED decides the fix:
   · low ARID  -> the climate model does not think it is dry. A climate problem;
                  building irrigation harder cannot help, the premium never applies.
   · low WATER -> the rivers do not resolve into channel magnitude here. A GRID
                  problem before it is a physics one — re-measure at tw=480 before
                  touching anything (third cardinal rule).
   · low TECH  -> the development wave has not arrived; the whole stack is gated
                  off regardless of climate or water. A timing problem.

